    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A model-free algorithm used to decompose linear combinations of signals with delays to identify source waveforms and locations.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A process that recovers hidden independent sources from a set of observed nonlinear mixtures.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A signal processing method that decomposes a composite signal into its constituent source components without prior knowledge of the mixing process.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A signal processing task that aims to recover independent source signals from a set of observed mixtures without prior knowledge of the mixing process.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A signal processing task that recovers independent source signals from a set of observed mixtures.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A signal processing task that recovers individual source signals from a mixture without knowing the mixing process.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A signal processing task that separates individual sound sources from a mixed audio signal without prior knowledge of the source signals.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A signal processing task that uses adversarial constraints to separate mixed signals into their original independent sources.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A signal processing technique that recovers individual source signals from a set of mixed observations without knowing the mixing process.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A signal processing technique that recovers latent source signals from a set of observed linear mixtures.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A signal processing technique that recovers original source signals from a set of mixed observations without knowing the mixing process.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A signal processing technique that recovers original source signals from a set of mixed observations without prior knowledge of the mixing process.</skos:definition>
