    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A data labeling approach that uses predefined rule-based labeling functions to automatically annotate samples.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A framework that uses noisy labeling functions, such as prompts from large language models, to train a classifier when labeled data is scarce.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A labeling paradigm that uses domain-knowledge heuristics to automatically generate noisy annotations for training data.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A labeling strategy that uses noisy or heuristic sources to generate training data instead of expensive manual annotation.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A labeling strategy that uses noisy sources or heuristics to generate training data for a model.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A learning approach that generates large amounts of labeled data using noisy, high-level heuristics instead of manual annotation.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A learning approach that leverages noisy or imprecise labels to train models when high-quality labeled data is scarce.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A learning approach that uses multiple human-designed heuristics to infer probabilistic label estimates without ground truth labels.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A learning approach that uses noisy or imprecise labeling sources to train models instead of relying on ground truth annotations.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A learning approach that uses noisy or imprecise labels created by heuristics to train a model when high-quality labels are scarce.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A learning approach that uses noisy or imprecise labels generated by heuristics or multiple sources to train a model.</skos:definition>
