  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://linqubit.co/ontology/ml_concepts#minimum_description_length">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Minimum Description Length</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A formalization of Occam's razor that selects the model that provides the best compression of the data.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A model selection principle that chooses the representation that minimizes the combined length of the model and the data.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A model selection principle that uses the shortest description of data to determine the most appropriate model complexity.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A principle for model selection that balances the complexity of the model against its ability to compress the data.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A principle for model selection that favors the hypothesis that provides the shortest overall description of the data and the model.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A principle for model selection that favors the model that provides the shortest overall description of the data and itself.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A principle for model selection that favors the shortest representation of the data.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A principle for model selection that favors the simplest explanation that can compress the data most effectively.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A principle for model selection that favors the simplest model that can adequately explain the given data.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A principle for model selection that seeks the shortest code to represent a given set of data.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A principle stating that the best model for a given set of data is the one that achieves the greatest compression of that data.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A principle that favors the simplest explanation for a set of data by minimizing the sum of the length of the model and the length of the data encoded by the model.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A principle that selects the model providing the shortest combined description of the model and the data.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A principle used as a criterion for model selection in linear dynamical systems to determine the optimal latent dimension.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A principle used for model selection that favors the simplest explanation that adequately summarizes a given set of data.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A principle used to define a measure of motif relevance in networks without requiring samples from a null model.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A statistical principle that evaluates model complexity based on the shortest possible encoding of the data and the model.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A theoretical framework that uses the shortest program length as a proxy to analyze the utility of specific data capabilities.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">An inductive principle that selects the model which provides the shortest combined description of the model and the data.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">An information theoretic principle that selects the model which provides the shortest overall encoding of the data.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">An information-theoretic principle for model selection that can be inconsistent when applied to certain classification problems.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">An information-theoretic principle for model selection that favors the model that provides the shortest overall description of the data.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">An information-theoretic principle for model selection that favors the shortest representation of the data.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">An information-theoretic principle for model selection that provides bounds on Hellinger losses for countable model classes.</rdfs:comment>
