  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://linqubit.co/ontology/ml_concepts#meta_reinforcement_learning">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Meta-Reinforcement Learning</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A learning approach that enables a controller to quickly adapt its policy to changing conditions by leveraging a library of prior policies.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A learning approach that enables agents to quickly adapt to new tasks by leveraging experience gained from related tasks.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A learning approach that enables an agent to adapt a single policy to solve a wide range of diverse locomotion tasks.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A learning approach that leverages experience from previous tasks to accelerate the acquisition of new skills in reinforcement learning.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A learning approach that trains a meta-learner to quickly adapt a policy to novel tasks by capturing shared and specific information.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A learning approach that trains a model on a variety of tasks to enable rapid adaptation to new, unseen tasks.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A learning approach that trains an agent to adapt quickly to new tasks by learning how to learn from previous experiences.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A learning approach that trains an agent to quickly adapt its policy to new, unseen tasks using a small number of samples.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A learning approach where an agent learns how to learn new tasks by optimizing its adaptation process across multiple environments.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A learning framework that enables an agent to adapt quickly to new tasks by learning a common initialization across a distribution of tasks.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A learning framework that enables an agent to adapt quickly to new tasks by learning how to learn from a small number of examples.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A learning framework that enables an agent to adapt quickly to new tasks by leveraging experience from a distribution of previous tasks.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A learning framework that identifies common structures across tasks to enable an agent to adapt quickly to new, unseen tasks.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A learning paradigm that enables an agent to adapt its policy quickly to new tasks by leveraging experience from previous environments.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A learning paradigm that enables an agent to adapt its policy to new tasks rapidly by inferring task identity from trajectories.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A learning paradigm that enables an agent to adapt quickly to new tasks by leveraging experience from a distribution of previous tasks.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A learning paradigm that enables an agent to learn how to learn by adapting to new tasks based on experience from previous ones.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A learning paradigm where an agent is trained on a variety of tasks to acquire a prior that enables rapid adaptation to new, unseen tasks.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A learning paradigm where an agent learns how to learn by optimizing its ability to adapt to new tasks based on experience from previous tasks.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A learning procedure that enables an agent to adapt quickly to a distribution of tasks using a small number of examples.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A learning process where an agent is trained across multiple tasks to learn how to learn new tasks more efficiently.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A learning scheme that shifts the objective from solving a specific task to learning how to learn to solve a set of tasks.</rdfs:comment>
