  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://linqubit.co/ontology/ml_concepts#minimax_optimization">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Minimax Optimization</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A decision rule used to minimize the maximum possible loss of a given strategy.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A mathematical framework for solving problems involving the simultaneous minimization of one objective and maximization of another.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A mathematical framework for solving problems where one player seeks to minimize a function while another seeks to maximize it.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A mathematical framework used to find a saddle point between two opposing objective functions.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A mathematical framework used to find a saddle point of a function that is minimized with respect to one variable and maximized with respect to another.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A mathematical framework used to minimize the maximum possible loss, often employed in adversarial training.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A mathematical problem involving the search for a saddle point in a function that is simultaneously minimized by one variable and maximized by another.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A mathematical problem involving the simultaneous minimization of one variable and maximization of another in a objective function.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A mathematical problem involving the simultaneous minimization of one variable and maximization of another, often used in adversarial training.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A mathematical problem of finding a saddle point in a function where one player seeks to minimize and another seeks to maximize the objective.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A mathematical process of finding a saddle point in a sequential game involving two players with opposing goals.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A mathematical process used in generative adversarial network training to find a saddle point between two competing objective functions.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A mathematical strategy used to minimize the maximum possible loss in a worst-case scenario, often applied in adversarial settings.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A mathematical strategy used to minimize the maximum possible loss, often employed to enhance the robustness of classifiers against adversarial attacks.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">[minimize the maximum possible loss of a given strategy, minimize the maximum possible loss, minimize the maximum possible loss in a worst-case scenario] minimize the maximum possible loss</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">[find a saddle point between two opposing objective functions, find a saddle point of a function that is minimized with respect to one variable and maximized with respect to another] find a saddle point between two opposing objective functions</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">[problems involving simultaneous minimization and maximization] problems involving simultaneous minimization and maximization</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">[one variable and another] one variable and another</rdfs:comment>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A decision rule used to minimize the maximum possible loss of a given strategy.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A mathematical framework for solving problems involving the simultaneous minimization of one objective and maximization of another.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A mathematical framework for solving problems where one player seeks to minimize a function while another seeks to maximize it.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A mathematical framework used to find a saddle point between two opposing objective functions.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A mathematical framework used to find a saddle point of a function that is minimized with respect to one variable and maximized with respect to another.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A mathematical framework used to minimize the maximum possible loss, often employed in adversarial training.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A mathematical problem involving the search for a saddle point in a function that is simultaneously minimized by one variable and maximized by another.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A mathematical problem involving the simultaneous minimization of one variable and maximization of another in a objective function.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A mathematical problem involving the simultaneous minimization of one variable and maximization of another, often used in adversarial training.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A mathematical problem of finding a saddle point in a function where one player seeks to minimize and another seeks to maximize the objective.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A mathematical process of finding a saddle point in a sequential game involving two players with opposing goals.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A mathematical process used in generative adversarial network training to find a saddle point between two competing objective functions.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A mathematical strategy used to minimize the maximum possible loss in a worst-case scenario, often applied in adversarial settings.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A mathematical strategy used to minimize the maximum possible loss, often employed to enhance the robustness of classifiers against adversarial attacks.</skos:definition>
