  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://linqubit.co/ontology/ml_concepts#out_of_distribution_generalization">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Out-Of-Distribution Generalization</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A capability of a model to maintain performance when applied to data that differs from the training distribution.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A capability of a model to maintain predictive accuracy when processing data that differs from the distribution seen during training.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A learning objective aimed at maximizing model performance on target domains that differ from the training domains.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A machine learning capability that allows a model to maintain performance on data from environments different from those seen during training.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A machine learning capability that allows a model to maintain performance when test data comes from a different distribution than the training data.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A model property that describes the ability to maintain performance on category-viewpoint combinations not encountered during training.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A performance metric that assesses a model's ability to maintain accuracy when encountering data from a different distribution than the training set.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A research problem focused on ensuring model performance when the testing distribution differs from the training distribution.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">The ability of a machine learning model to maintain performance when applied to data from a domain different from the training set.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">The ability of a machine learning model to maintain performance when applied to data from environments different from the training set.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">The ability of a machine learning model to maintain performance when applied to data that comes from a different distribution than the training set.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">The ability of a machine learning model to maintain performance when applied to data that differs from the training distribution.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">The ability of a machine learning model to maintain performance when encountering data that differs from the training distribution.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">The ability of a machine learning model to maintain performance when test data comes from a different distribution than the training data.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">The ability of a machine learning model to maintain performance when test data comes from a different distribution than training data.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">The ability of a machine learning model to maintain performance when the deployment data distribution differs from the training data distribution.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">The ability of a machine learning model to maintain performance when the test data comes from a different distribution than the training data.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">The ability of a machine learning model to maintain performance when the test data distribution differs from the training data distribution.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">The ability of a machine learning model to maintain performance when the test data distribution differs from the training distribution.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">The ability of a model to maintain performance on data that differs from the distribution encountered during training.</rdfs:comment>
