    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A reinforcement learning strategy that uses a sequence of increasingly difficult imaginary object goals to train robot manipulation in sparse reward environments.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A training methodology that accelerates the acquisition of a complex target task by sequentially training on a series of simpler tasks.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A training strategy in deep reinforcement learning where an agent progresses through tasks of increasing difficulty.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A training strategy that adjusts the difficulty or weight of samples over time to improve model robustness.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A training strategy that arranges training examples in a hierarchy of increasing difficulty to improve model convergence and performance.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A training strategy that arranges training samples in order of increasing difficulty to improve agent performance and efficiency.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A training strategy that decomposes a complex target task into a sequence of increasingly difficult source tasks.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A training strategy that decomposes complex tasks into a sequence of simpler intermediate goals to incrementally build model skills.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A training strategy that dynamically adjusts the difficulty or weight of learning modules to improve generalization in imbalanced node classification.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A training strategy that expedites the learning of complex tasks by presenting examples in an increasing order of difficulty.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A training strategy that fine-tunes a generative network by presenting tasks in an increasing order of difficulty to satisfy multiple objectives.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A training strategy that gradually concentrates on multi-domain relevant and noise-reduced data batches to improve machine translation.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A training strategy that gradually increases the complexity of tasks to improve model generalization.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A training strategy that gradually increases the complexity of tasks to improve the convergence of a model.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A training strategy that gradually increases the difficulty of training data or objectives to improve model performance on complex tasks.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A training strategy that gradually progresses the task distribution from simple to complex to improve sample efficiency in reinforcement learning.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A training strategy that guides the learning process by presenting training examples in an order of increasing difficulty.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A training strategy that improves model effectiveness by presenting training instances in an order from easy to difficult.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A training strategy that improves model generalization by presenting training examples in an increasing order of difficulty.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A training strategy that improves model performance by gradually exposing the learner to examples ordered from easy to hard.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A training strategy that increases the complexity of the learning task progressively to improve model convergence and performance.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A training strategy that introduces data samples to a model in an order of increasing difficulty.</skos:definition>
