  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://linqubit.co/ontology/ml_concepts#differentiable_programming">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Differentiable Programming</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A computational paradigm where programs are represented as neural networks that can be optimized via gradient descent.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A paradigm that combines neural networks with programming languages to create end-to-end trainable systems that learn interpretable algorithms.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A paradigm that treats programs as differentiable functions to enable optimization via automatic gradient calculation.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A paradigm where the computational pipeline is expressed as a set of differentiable operations amenable to gradient-based optimization.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A programming paradigm implemented in the Flux framework that allows for the refinement of machine learning core ideas through a performant environment.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A programming paradigm that allows the calculation of gradients through a set of operations, including the solutions of optimization problems.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A programming paradigm that allows the computation of gradients for general program structures using automatic differentiation.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A programming paradigm that enables the computation of gradients of computer programs for flexible statistical modeling.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A programming paradigm where a computer program can be differentiated automatically to optimize its parameters via gradient descent.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A programming paradigm where programs are written as a sequence of differentiable operations, allowing them to be optimized via gradient descent.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A software paradigm that allows for the computation of gradients through a program, used here to solve the inverse problem of phase retrieval in microscopy.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A software paradigm that allows gradients to be computed through a program to optimize its parameters.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A software paradigm that allows numerical gradients to be computed through the execution of a program.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A software paradigm that allows the computation of gradients of a program with respect to its parameters using automatic differentiation.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A software paradigm that allows the computation of gradients through numerical routines for integration into neural networks.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A software paradigm that enables the use of gradient-based optimization on functions by making them differentiable.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A software paradigm that integrates domain-specific simulation code into gradient-based learning and optimization pipelines via automatic differentiation.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A software paradigm where programs are written as differentiable functions to allow gradient-based optimization of their parameters.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A software paradigm where the entire computational pipeline is expressed as a differentiable function for optimization.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">[programs] programs</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">[parameters/functions] parameters/functions</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">[neural networks with programming languages] neural networks with programming languages</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">[machine learning core ideas] machine learning core ideas</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">[gradients] gradient</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">[simulation code] simulation code</rdfs:comment>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A computational paradigm where programs are represented as neural networks that can be optimized via gradient descent.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A paradigm that combines neural networks with programming languages to create end-to-end trainable systems that learn interpretable algorithms.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A paradigm that treats programs as differentiable functions to enable optimization via automatic gradient calculation.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A paradigm where the computational pipeline is expressed as a set of differentiable operations amenable to gradient-based optimization.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A programming paradigm implemented in the Flux framework that allows for the refinement of machine learning core ideas through a performant environment.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A programming paradigm that allows the calculation of gradients through a set of operations, including the solutions of optimization problems.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A programming paradigm that allows the computation of gradients for general program structures using automatic differentiation.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A programming paradigm that enables the computation of gradients of computer programs for flexible statistical modeling.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A programming paradigm where a computer program can be differentiated automatically to optimize its parameters via gradient descent.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A programming paradigm where programs are written as a sequence of differentiable operations, allowing them to be optimized via gradient descent.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A software paradigm that allows for the computation of gradients through a program, used here to solve the inverse problem of phase retrieval in microscopy.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A software paradigm that allows gradients to be computed through a program to optimize its parameters.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A software paradigm that allows numerical gradients to be computed through the execution of a program.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A software paradigm that allows the computation of gradients of a program with respect to its parameters using automatic differentiation.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A software paradigm that allows the computation of gradients through numerical routines for integration into neural networks.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A software paradigm that enables the use of gradient-based optimization on functions by making them differentiable.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A software paradigm that integrates domain-specific simulation code into gradient-based learning and optimization pipelines via automatic differentiation.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A software paradigm where programs are written as differentiable functions to allow gradient-based optimization of their parameters.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A software paradigm where the entire computational pipeline is expressed as a differentiable function for optimization.</skos:definition>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://linqubit.co/ontology/ml_concepts#computational_frameworks"/>
    <ml:hasComponent rdf:resource="http://linqubit.co/ontology/ml_concepts#automatic_differentiation"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://linqubit.co/ontology/ml_concepts#adversarialmultiarmedbandit">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Adversarialmultiarmedbandit</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A decision problem where a player chooses actions to minimize regret against a worst-case adversary.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A decision-making framework that optimizes reward in environments with graph-based feedback and adversarial losses.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A decision-making framework where an agent chooses actions to minimize cumulative loss against an adaptive opponent with partial feedback.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A decision-making framework where an agent chooses actions to minimize regret against an arbitrary sequence of losses.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A decision-making framework where an agent selects actions to minimize regret in an environment with potentially adversarial rewards and switching costs.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A decision-making framework where reward distributions are chosen by an opponent to minimize the agent's cumulative gain.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A decision-making model that optimizes the trade-off between exploration and exploitation in a non-stochastic environment.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A decision-making problem where an agent must choose between multiple options to maximize reward in an environment where rewards are chosen by an adversary.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A decision-making problem where an agent must choose between multiple options to minimize regret against an adaptive opponent.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A decision-making problem where an agent must choose between multiple options with rewards controlled by an adversary to minimize regret.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A reinforcement learning framework used to optimize decision-making in an environment where rewards may be non-stationary or adversarial.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">An online algorithmic framework for decision making under uncertainty that is invariant under arbitrary translations and scales of arm losses.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">An online learning algorithm that optimizes selection decisions under uncertainty and potential adversarial attacks.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">An online learning framework used for task assignment in dynamic environments without making stochastic assumptions about the system.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">[optimize decision-making in an environment where rewards may be non-stationary or adversarial] optimize decision-making in an environment where rewards may be non-stationary or adversarial</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">[task assignment in dynamic environments without making stochastic assumptions about the system] task assignment in dynamic environments without making stochastic assumptions about the system</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">[cumulative loss] cumulative loss</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">[cumulative gain] cumulative gain</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">[reward] reward</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">[trade-off between exploration and exploitation] exploration and exploitation</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">[selection decisions] selection decisions</rdfs:comment>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A decision problem where a player chooses actions to minimize regret against a worst-case adversary.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A decision-making framework that optimizes reward in environments with graph-based feedback and adversarial losses.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A decision-making framework where an agent chooses actions to minimize cumulative loss against an adaptive opponent with partial feedback.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A decision-making framework where an agent chooses actions to minimize regret against an arbitrary sequence of losses.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A decision-making framework where an agent selects actions to minimize regret in an environment with potentially adversarial rewards and switching costs.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A decision-making framework where reward distributions are chosen by an opponent to minimize the agent's cumulative gain.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A decision-making model that optimizes the trade-off between exploration and exploitation in a non-stochastic environment.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A decision-making problem where an agent must choose between multiple options to maximize reward in an environment where rewards are chosen by an adversary.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A decision-making problem where an agent must choose between multiple options to minimize regret against an adaptive opponent.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A decision-making problem where an agent must choose between multiple options with rewards controlled by an adversary to minimize regret.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A reinforcement learning framework used to optimize decision-making in an environment where rewards may be non-stationary or adversarial.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">An online algorithmic framework for decision making under uncertainty that is invariant under arbitrary translations and scales of arm losses.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">An online learning algorithm that optimizes selection decisions under uncertainty and potential adversarial attacks.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">An online learning framework used for task assignment in dynamic environments without making stochastic assumptions about the system.</skos:definition>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://linqubit.co/ontology/ml_concepts#bandit_learning_task"/>
    <ml:optimizes rdf:resource="http://linqubit.co/ontology/ml_concepts#regret"/>
  </rdf:Description>
