    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A mathematical framework used to model the dynamics of systems that evolve over time with intrinsic randomness.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A mathematical framework used to model the evolution of a random covariance matrix in infinite-depth neural networks.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A mathematical framework used to model the evolution of systems subject to random noise over time.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A mathematical model that describes systems with abrupt or jumping behaviors under non-Gaussian noise.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A mathematical model that describes the evolution of a system over time incorporating both deterministic and random components.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A mathematical model that describes the evolution of a system subject to random fluctuations.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A mathematical model that describes the evolution of a system subject to random noise.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A mathematical model used as a basis for generative time series models to estimate drift and volatility parameters.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A mathematical model used to approximate the training and generalization dynamics of neural networks.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A mathematical model used to approximate the training process of residual networks with noise injection.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A mathematical model used to characterize the second-order moments of non-uniformly sampled time series data.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A mathematical model used to describe the evolution of features during the training of deep learning models using stochastic gradient descent.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A mathematical representation of a dynamical system that incorporates both deterministic and random components.</skos:definition>
