  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://linqubit.co/ontology/ml_concepts#deep_transfer_learning">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Deep Transfer Learning</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A machine learning approach that applies knowledge gained from one task or domain to a different but related task or domain.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A machine learning approach that applies knowledge gained from one task to a different but related task to improve performance.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A machine learning approach that leverages knowledge from a source domain to improve learning in a target domain using deep neural networks.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A machine learning method that applies knowledge gained from one task to a different but related task to improve learning efficiency.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A machine learning method that applies knowledge gained from one task to a different but related task to improve performance.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A machine learning paradigm that leverages knowledge from a source domain to improve learning performance in a target domain with limited labeled data.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A machine learning technique that applies knowledge from a model trained on one task to a different but related task.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A machine learning technique that applies knowledge gained from a data-rich source domain to a resource-constrained target domain.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A machine learning technique that applies knowledge gained from one task or domain to a different but related task or domain.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A machine learning technique that applies knowledge gained from one task to a different but related task to improve learning efficiency.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A machine learning technique that applies knowledge gained from one task to a different but related task using deep neural networks.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A machine learning technique that applies knowledge gained from one task to a different but related task.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A machine learning technique that applies knowledge gained while solving one problem to a different but related problem.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A machine learning technique that applies knowledge learned from one domain to improve performance in a related but different target domain.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A machine learning technique that leverages a pre-trained model from a source domain to improve performance on a target task with limited data.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A machine learning technique where a model developed for one task is reused as the starting point for a model on a second task.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A method that applies knowledge gained from a source task to improve learning in a related target domain.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A method that leverages knowledge from a pre-trained model on a source task to improve learning on a target task.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A methodology that applies knowledge acquired from one task or domain to improve the performance of a model on a different but related task.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A methodology that applies knowledge from one domain, such as visual recognition, to another, such as multi-corpus speech emotion recognition.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A methodology that applies knowledge learned from non-membrane proteins to predict the structures of membrane proteins.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A methodology that leverages knowledge from a large source dataset to improve the performance of a model on a target task with limited data.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A methodology that transfers knowledge from a large source dataset to a small target cohort to overcome data scarcity and channel mismatch.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A methodology where a model trained on one domain is adapted to improve performance on a different but related target task.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A process of leveraging knowledge from a pre-trained deep neural network to improve learning on a different but related dataset.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A technique that applies a pre-trained model from one task to a different but related target task to improve learning efficiency.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A technique that applies knowledge gained from a source domain to a target domain where labeled data is limited.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A technique that applies knowledge gained from one domain to a different but related task to improve learning with limited data.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A technique that applies knowledge gained from one task to a different but related task to improve classification accuracy.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A technique that applies knowledge gained from one task to a different but related task to improve detection performance.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A technique that leverages knowledge from a pre-trained model to improve learning on a different but related task.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A technique that leverages knowledge from a pre-trained network to improve the performance of a target task, such as behavior recognition from speech.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A technique that leverages knowledge gained from one task to improve the learning of a different but related task using deep neural networks.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A technique where a model developed for one task is reused as the starting point for a model on a second, related task to handle data scarcity.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A technique where a model developed for one task is reused as the starting point for a model on a second, related task.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A technique where a pre-trained convolutional neural network is adapted to a new task to achieve high performance with small datasets.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">[knowledge gained, knowledge, knowledge gained while solving one problem, knowledge learned, knowledge acquired] knowledge</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">[source domain/model/dataset] source dataset</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">[model] model</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">[source dataset] source dataset</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">[a model] model</rdfs:comment>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A machine learning approach that applies knowledge gained from one task or domain to a different but related task or domain.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A machine learning approach that applies knowledge gained from one task to a different but related task to improve performance.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A machine learning approach that leverages knowledge from a source domain to improve learning in a target domain using deep neural networks.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A machine learning method that applies knowledge gained from one task to a different but related task to improve learning efficiency.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A machine learning method that applies knowledge gained from one task to a different but related task to improve performance.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A machine learning paradigm that leverages knowledge from a source domain to improve learning performance in a target domain with limited labeled data.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A machine learning technique that applies knowledge from a model trained on one task to a different but related task.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A machine learning technique that applies knowledge gained from a data-rich source domain to a resource-constrained target domain.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A machine learning technique that applies knowledge gained from one task or domain to a different but related task or domain.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A machine learning technique that applies knowledge gained from one task to a different but related task to improve learning efficiency.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A machine learning technique that applies knowledge gained from one task to a different but related task using deep neural networks.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A machine learning technique that applies knowledge gained from one task to a different but related task.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A machine learning technique that applies knowledge gained while solving one problem to a different but related problem.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A machine learning technique that applies knowledge learned from one domain to improve performance in a related but different target domain.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A machine learning technique that leverages a pre-trained model from a source domain to improve performance on a target task with limited data.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A machine learning technique where a model developed for one task is reused as the starting point for a model on a second task.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A method that applies knowledge gained from a source task to improve learning in a related target domain.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A method that leverages knowledge from a pre-trained model on a source task to improve learning on a target task.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A methodology that applies knowledge acquired from one task or domain to improve the performance of a model on a different but related task.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A methodology that applies knowledge from one domain, such as visual recognition, to another, such as multi-corpus speech emotion recognition.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A methodology that applies knowledge learned from non-membrane proteins to predict the structures of membrane proteins.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A methodology that leverages knowledge from a large source dataset to improve the performance of a model on a target task with limited data.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A methodology that transfers knowledge from a large source dataset to a small target cohort to overcome data scarcity and channel mismatch.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A methodology where a model trained on one domain is adapted to improve performance on a different but related target task.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A process of leveraging knowledge from a pre-trained deep neural network to improve learning on a different but related dataset.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A technique that applies a pre-trained model from one task to a different but related target task to improve learning efficiency.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A technique that applies knowledge gained from a source domain to a target domain where labeled data is limited.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A technique that applies knowledge gained from one domain to a different but related task to improve learning with limited data.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A technique that applies knowledge gained from one task to a different but related task to improve classification accuracy.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A technique that applies knowledge gained from one task to a different but related task to improve detection performance.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A technique that leverages knowledge from a pre-trained model to improve learning on a different but related task.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A technique that leverages knowledge from a pre-trained network to improve the performance of a target task, such as behavior recognition from speech.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A technique that leverages knowledge gained from one task to improve the learning of a different but related task using deep neural networks.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A technique where a model developed for one task is reused as the starting point for a model on a second, related task to handle data scarcity.</skos:definition>
