    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A task that identifies anomalous data samples that differ from the known training distribution using latent space distance measures.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A task that identifies data points that do not belong to the distribution of the training data.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A task that identifies data samples that fall outside the training data distribution to prevent confidently wrong predictions.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A task that identifies images not belonging to the training distribution using temperature scaling and input perturbations.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A task that identifies input examples that do not belong to the distribution of the training data to prevent confident incorrect predictions.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A task that identifies input samples that differ significantly from the training data distribution to avoid overconfident incorrect predictions.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A task that identifies input samples that differ significantly from the training data distribution.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A task that identifies input samples that do not belong to any of the classes seen during the training phase.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A task that identifies input samples that do not belong to the distribution of the data used to train the model.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A task that identifies input samples that do not belong to the distribution of the training data to avoid overconfident predictions.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A task that identifies input samples that do not belong to the distribution of the training data to ensure system reliability.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A task that identifies input samples that do not belong to the distribution of the training data.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A task that identifies inputs that differ significantly from the data distribution used to train a model.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A task that identifies inputs that do not belong to the distribution of the training data.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A task that identifies inputs that do not belong to the training data distribution, here implemented via hierarchical outlier detection for skin conditions.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A task that identifies inputs that do not belong to the training distribution of a deep model.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A task that identifies inputs that significantly differ from the training data to ensure system robustness.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A task that identifies samples falling outside the semantic space of a trained deep neural network.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A task that identifies samples not belonging to the training distribution by combining density and restoration-based approaches.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A task that identifies samples that diverge from the training data distribution to improve model reliability.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A task that identifies samples that do not belong to the distribution of the training data.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A task that identifies samples that do not belong to the training data distribution during inference.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A task that identifies samples that do not belong to the training distribution by exploiting in-batch dependencies in generative models.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A task that identifies test samples that are drawn from a distribution different from the training data.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A task that identifies test samples that do not belong to the distribution of the training data.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A task that identifies when a model receives an input that does not belong to the distribution of its training data.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A task that identifies whether a given input example is drawn from the same distribution as the training data.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A task that identifies whether a given input sample belongs to the same distribution as the training data or comes from a different one.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A task that identifies whether a given input sample belongs to the target training distribution or is a novelty.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A task that identifies whether a given input sample belongs to the training data distribution or is an outlier.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A task that identifies whether a given input sample belongs to the training data distribution or represents a novel class.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A task that identifies whether a given input sample belongs to the training distribution or is an outlier.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A task that identifies whether a given input sample belongs to the training distribution or to an unseen class.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A task that identifies whether a given input sample comes from the same distribution as the training data.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A task that identifies whether a given input sample comes from the same probability distribution as the training data.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A task that identifies whether a given input sample deviates from the distribution of data used during the model's training.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A task that identifies whether a given input sample deviates from the distribution of the training data.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A task that identifies whether a given input sample differs significantly from the data used during training.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A task that identifies whether a given input sample originates from a distribution different from the one used during training.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A task that identifies whether a given input sample originates from the same distribution as the training data.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A task that identifies whether a test sample comes from a different distribution than the training data.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A task that identifies whether a test sample originates from a different distribution than the training data.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A task that identifies whether a test sample originates from the same distribution as the training data.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A task that identifies whether an input sample belongs to the training distribution or is an outlier.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A task that identifies whether an input sample deviates from the distribution of the training data.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A task that identifies whether an input sample differs significantly from the data used during training.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A task that identifies whether an input sample originates from a distribution different from the one used during model training.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A task that identifies whether inputs are valid for the problem a neural network is solving by analyzing predictive uncertainty.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A task that involves identifying data points that differ significantly from the training distribution to ensure model reliability in safety-critical applications.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A task that involves identifying whether a given input sample comes from the same distribution as the training data.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A task where a model determines if an input sample originates from a distribution different from its training data.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A technique used to evaluate the generalization of control policies trained in simulation before deploying them on real robots.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A technique used to identify data points that do not belong to the distribution of the training set to improve model fairness and generalization.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A technique used to identify input data that differs significantly from the distribution of the training set.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">An evaluation process that identifies a model's failure to generalize when exposed to data from a different distribution than the training set.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">An uncertainty quantification process that identifies input samples that differ significantly from the training data distribution.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">An unsupervised methodology that identifies images that do not belong to the training distribution using score estimate norms.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">The process of identifying input samples that do not belong to the distribution of the training data.</skos:definition>
