  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://linqubit.co/ontology/ml_concepts#adversarial_learning">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Adversarial Learning</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A bias mitigation technique that uses a discriminator to ensure sensitive attributes cannot be distinguished during classification.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A field of machine learning focused on the security evaluation and robustness of models against targeted malicious attacks.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A field of research focused on creating and defending against perturbations designed to deceive machine learning models.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A field of study focused on attacks and defenses for machine learning models subject to malicious input perturbations.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A framework that uses a game-theoretic approach to align embeddings from different knowledge graphs in an unsupervised manner.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A framework that uses a minimax game between an encoder and an adversary to decouple sensitive attributes from data representations.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A framework that uses competing networks to mask private information while preserving utility for a target prediction task.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A framework using a generator and discriminator to optimize a trade-off between data privacy and utility.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A framework where two neural networks, a generator and a discriminator, compete to improve the quality of generated data.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A machine learning approach that uses a game-theoretic framework to train a model by pitting it against an adversary.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A machine learning approach that uses competing networks to perform nonparametric estimation of event-time distributions in survival analysis.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A machine learning framework where two networks compete to improve the quality of synthetic data generation.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A machine learning paradigm designed to harden models against malicious evasion attacks.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A machine learning paradigm that trains models to be robust against carefully crafted perturbations designed to cause errors.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A machine learning paradigm that trains models to be robust against maliciously crafted input perturbations.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A machine learning paradigm that uses a competitive process between two networks to improve the accuracy of optical flow tracking in satellite imagery.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A machine learning paradigm that uses a game-theoretic approach to improve model performance by training a generator and a discriminator.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A machine learning paradigm that utilizes a competitive process between two networks to learn target density functions.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A machine learning paradigm where two networks are trained simultaneously in a game-theoretic manner to optimize competing objectives.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A machine learning process where a model is trained to resist or detect attempts by an opponent to reverse engineer its internal dynamics.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A machine learning technique that uses augmented samples or latent features to improve the robustness and performance of a model.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A machine learning technique used to develop fair risk prediction models by aligning distribution of predictions across different groups.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A machine learning technique where a model is trained to resist or generate deceptive inputs to test system robustness.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A technique used to identify or defend against small, intentional perturbations to input data that cause a model to make mistakes.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A training approach utilizing a min-max game between a generator and a discriminator to solve inverse problems.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A training framework that employs competing networks to improve the robustness and interpretability of estimated patterns.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A training framework that minimizes identity-specific information while maximizing emotion-dependent information for privacy-aware recognition.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A training framework that uses a competing network to remove sensitive or private information from data representations.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A training framework that uses competitive encoding distributions to learn robust representations of data.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A training framework where two networks compete against each other to improve the quality of generated data or learned representations.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A training framework where two networks compete against each other to learn robust feature representations.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A training framework where two neural networks compete against each other to improve the performance of a generative or discriminative model.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A training framework where two neural networks, a generator and a discriminator, compete against each other to learn complex data distributions.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A training methodology that improves model robustness by incorporating adversarial examples into the learning process.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A training paradigm focused on making models robust against intentionally perturbed input data.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A training paradigm that aims to make models robust to small, intentional perturbations of the input data.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A training paradigm that incorporates adversarial examples to improve the robustness of a model against malicious perturbations.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A training paradigm that learns representations invariant to specific attributes by using a discriminator to oppose the primary task.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A training paradigm that uses a competing agent to create perturbations that challenge a model's robustness or security.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A training paradigm that uses a discriminator to encourage a model to learn domain-invariant representations.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A training paradigm that uses a discriminator to guide a generator toward producing data that is indistinguishable from a target distribution.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A training paradigm that uses a discriminator to improve the performance of a generator or agent without a simulator.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A training paradigm that uses competing networks to improve model robustness or remove specific biases.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A training paradigm using a min-max game to improve the robustness of hashing networks against malicious perturbations.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A training paradigm where two networks compete against each other to improve the performance of a specific objective.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A training paradigm where two networks compete against each other to improve the robustness or fairness of a model.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A training paradigm where two networks, a generator and a discriminator, are trained simultaneously in a competitive game to improve model performance.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A training paradigm where two neural networks compete against each other to improve the performance of a generative model.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A training paradigm where two neural networks compete against each other to improve the performance of a generative or discriminative model.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A training paradigm where two neural networks compete against each other to improve the quality of generated data or feature representations.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A training paradigm where two neural networks, a generator and a discriminator, compete against each other to improve the quality of generated data.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A training process that incorporates adversarial examples to improve the robustness of a model against malicious perturbations.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A training process that optimizes a model to be robust against worst-case perturbations of the input data.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A training process where two neural networks, a generator and a discriminator, compete against each other to improve the performance of a specific task.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A training strategy that uses a competitive process between two models to improve the robustness or accuracy of a predictor.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A training strategy that uses a discriminator network to regularize image registration by ensuring predicted deformations are physically plausible.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A training strategy that utilizes a competing network to remove specific information, such as sensitive attributes, from a latent representation.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A training strategy where two neural networks compete against each other to improve the performance of a generative model.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A training strategy where two neural networks, a generator and a discriminator, compete against each other to improve the quality of generated data.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A training technique that uses adversarial examples to improve the robustness of a model against malicious perturbations.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A training technique where two networks compete against each other to learn robust or domain-invariant representations.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A training technique where two neural networks, a generator and a discriminator, compete against each other to improve the model's performance or close domain gaps.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A training technique where two or more neural networks compete against each other to improve the robustness or generative capabilities of the model.</rdfs:comment>
