    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A machine learning paradigm that trains models to be robust against carefully crafted perturbations designed to cause errors.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A machine learning paradigm that trains models to be robust against maliciously crafted input perturbations.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A machine learning paradigm that uses a competitive process between two networks to improve the accuracy of optical flow tracking in satellite imagery.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A machine learning paradigm that uses a game-theoretic approach to improve model performance by training a generator and a discriminator.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A machine learning paradigm that utilizes a competitive process between two networks to learn target density functions.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A machine learning paradigm where two networks are trained simultaneously in a game-theoretic manner to optimize competing objectives.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A machine learning process where a model is trained to resist or detect attempts by an opponent to reverse engineer its internal dynamics.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A machine learning technique that uses augmented samples or latent features to improve the robustness and performance of a model.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A machine learning technique used to develop fair risk prediction models by aligning distribution of predictions across different groups.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A machine learning technique where a model is trained to resist or generate deceptive inputs to test system robustness.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A technique used to identify or defend against small, intentional perturbations to input data that cause a model to make mistakes.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A training approach utilizing a min-max game between a generator and a discriminator to solve inverse problems.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A training framework that employs competing networks to improve the robustness and interpretability of estimated patterns.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A training framework that minimizes identity-specific information while maximizing emotion-dependent information for privacy-aware recognition.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A training framework that uses a competing network to remove sensitive or private information from data representations.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A training framework that uses competitive encoding distributions to learn robust representations of data.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A training framework where two networks compete against each other to improve the quality of generated data or learned representations.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A training framework where two networks compete against each other to learn robust feature representations.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A training framework where two neural networks compete against each other to improve the performance of a generative or discriminative model.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A training framework where two neural networks, a generator and a discriminator, compete against each other to learn complex data distributions.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A training methodology that improves model robustness by incorporating adversarial examples into the learning process.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A training paradigm focused on making models robust against intentionally perturbed input data.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A training paradigm that aims to make models robust to small, intentional perturbations of the input data.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A training paradigm that incorporates adversarial examples to improve the robustness of a model against malicious perturbations.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A training paradigm that learns representations invariant to specific attributes by using a discriminator to oppose the primary task.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A training paradigm that uses a competing agent to create perturbations that challenge a model's robustness or security.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A training paradigm that uses a discriminator to encourage a model to learn domain-invariant representations.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A training paradigm that uses a discriminator to guide a generator toward producing data that is indistinguishable from a target distribution.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A training paradigm that uses a discriminator to improve the performance of a generator or agent without a simulator.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A training paradigm that uses competing networks to improve model robustness or remove specific biases.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A training paradigm using a min-max game to improve the robustness of hashing networks against malicious perturbations.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A training paradigm where two networks compete against each other to improve the performance of a specific objective.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A training paradigm where two networks compete against each other to improve the robustness or fairness of a model.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A training paradigm where two networks, a generator and a discriminator, are trained simultaneously in a competitive game to improve model performance.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A training paradigm where two neural networks compete against each other to improve the performance of a generative model.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A training paradigm where two neural networks compete against each other to improve the performance of a generative or discriminative model.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A training paradigm where two neural networks compete against each other to improve the quality of generated data or feature representations.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A training paradigm where two neural networks, a generator and a discriminator, compete against each other to improve the quality of generated data.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A training process that incorporates adversarial examples to improve the robustness of a model against malicious perturbations.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A training process that optimizes a model to be robust against worst-case perturbations of the input data.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A training process where two neural networks, a generator and a discriminator, compete against each other to improve the performance of a specific task.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A training strategy that uses a competitive process between two models to improve the robustness or accuracy of a predictor.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A training strategy that uses a discriminator network to regularize image registration by ensuring predicted deformations are physically plausible.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A training strategy that utilizes a competing network to remove specific information, such as sensitive attributes, from a latent representation.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A training strategy where two neural networks compete against each other to improve the performance of a generative model.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A training strategy where two neural networks, a generator and a discriminator, compete against each other to improve the quality of generated data.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A training technique that uses adversarial examples to improve the robustness of a model against malicious perturbations.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A training technique where two networks compete against each other to learn robust or domain-invariant representations.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A training technique where two neural networks, a generator and a discriminator, compete against each other to improve the model's performance or close domain gaps.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A training technique where two or more neural networks compete against each other to improve the robustness or generative capabilities of the model.</skos:definition>
