  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://linqubit.co/ontology/ml_concepts#equivariantneuralnetwork">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Equivariantneuralnetwork</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A deep learning architecture that ensures the output transforms predictably according to specific symmetry groups.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A deep learning model that preserves specific symmetries or transformations of the input data to learn molecular representations.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A learning model that preserves the symmetry of the input data using reductive operators.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A model architecture designed to ensure that transformations of the input result in corresponding transformations of the output.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A model architecture designed to maintain specific transformation properties across its input and output spaces.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A model architecture that ensures the output transforms consistently with the input according to a specific group symmetry.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A model architecture that ensures the output transforms consistently with the input's rotations or translations.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A model architecture that ensures the output transforms in a consistent way when the input undergoes a specific symmetry transformation.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A model architecture that ensures the output transforms in the same way as the input under specific symmetry groups.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A model architecture that ensures the transformation of hidden features commutes with a specific group action on the input.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A model architecture that ensures transformations of the input result in predictable, corresponding transformations of the output.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A model architecture that preserves specific symmetry transformations of the input data in its output.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A model architecture that preserves the structural transformations of its input data across its internal operations.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A model architecture whose internal representations transform predictably in response to specific input transformations like rotation or scaling.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A model whose output transforms in the same way as its input under a group of transformations.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A network architecture that ensures the output transforms in a way that is consistent with a specific symmetry group of the input.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A network design that ensures the output transforms consistently with specific transformations applied to the input.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A neural architecture that ensures the output transforms in a consistent manner relative to a specific symmetry group action applied to the input.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A neural architecture that preserves specific symmetries in the input data to improve efficiency and accuracy.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A neural network architecture designed to be invariant or equivariant to specific symmetry groups, such as rotations or the Poincare group.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A neural network architecture designed to be invariant or equivariant under specific symmetry group transformations of the input data.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A neural network architecture that preserves the symmetry of the input data through its transformations.</rdfs:comment>
