    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A mathematical framework used to represent high-dimensional tensors as a network of smaller, interconnected tensors.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A mathematical structure used to represent high-dimensional tensors that can be mapped to deep feed-forward neural networks for quantum state approximation.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A mathematical tool from quantum physics used to represent and optimize policies in Markov decision processes.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A multilinear algebra tool used to compress high-dimensional tensors to improve the efficiency of machine learning models.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A neural architecture that simultaneously performs classification and sampling to characterize the properties of a data set.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A numerical framework that represents high-dimensional tensors as a contraction of smaller tensors to reduce computational complexity.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A parametrization method that represents a neural network as a single high-order low-rank tensor to reduce redundancy.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A quantum-inspired mathematical framework used to represent high-dimensional tensors as a network of smaller tensors to reduce parameters.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A restricted class of state representations used as classifiers to map classical data to classical data.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A sparse data structure used to represent and contract high-dimensional tensors efficiently.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A statistical model based on directed acyclic graphs decorated by multi-linear tensor maps used for learning systems with long distance correlations.</skos:definition>
