  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://linqubit.co/ontology/ml_concepts#triplet_loss">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Triplet Loss</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A distance metric learning objective that optimizes the embedding space so that an anchor is closer to a positive example than to a negative example.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A distance-based objective function that encourages a sample to be closer to a positive example than to a negative one.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A distance-based objective function that encourages similar samples to be closer and dissimilar samples to be farther apart in embedding space.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A learning method that compares anchor, positive, and negative examples to learn meaningful descriptors from 3D point clouds.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A loss function that encourages a model to minimize the distance between an anchor and a positive sample while maximizing the distance to a negative sample.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A loss function that encourages the embedding of a positive sample to be closer to an anchor than to a negative sample.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A loss function that encourages the embedding of a positive sample to be closer to the anchor than a negative sample.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A loss function that encourages the model to map similar items closer together and dissimilar items further apart in a embedding space.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A loss function that trains a neural network to project signals into an embedding space where Euclidean distance represents perceptual dissimilarity.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A loss function used in embedding learning that encourages a positive sample to be closer to an anchor than a negative sample.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A loss function used in metric learning to ensure that a positive pair is closer than a negative pair by a specified margin.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A loss function used in metric learning to ensure that a positive sample is closer to the anchor than a negative sample by a certain margin.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A loss function used in metric learning to ensure that a reference sample is closer to a positive sample than to a negative one.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A loss function used in metric learning to ensure that an anchor sample is closer to a positive sample than to a negative sample in embedding space.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A loss function used in representation learning to ensure that a given sample is closer to a positive sample than to a negative sample.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A loss function used in unsupervised representation learning to ensure that similar samples are closer together than dissimilar ones in embedding space.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A loss function used to learn a compact Euclidean embedding where distance represents similarity between images for open-set recognition.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A loss function used to optimize the Euclidean distances between speaker embeddings to improve text-independent speaker verification.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A loss function used to train neural networks to learn embeddings that minimize the distance between matching pairs and maximize it for non-matching pairs.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A metric learning loss function that encourages the distance between an anchor and a positive sample to be smaller than the distance between an anchor and a negative sample.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A ranking loss function that encourages a model to minimize the distance between an anchor and a positive sample while maximizing it from a negative sample.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A self-supervised loss function used to create alternative feature representations for acoustic modeling in automatic speech recognition.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">[learn a compact euclidean embedding where distance represents similarity between images for open-set recognition] learn a compact euclidean embedding where distance represents similarity between images for open-set recognition</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">[optimize the euclidean distances between speaker embeddings to improve text-independent speaker verification] optimize the euclidean distances between speaker embeddings to improve text-independent speaker verification</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">[train neural networks to learn embeddings that minimize the distance between matching pairs and maximize it for non-matching pairs] train neural networks to learn embeddings that minimize the distance between matching pairs and maximize it for non-matching pairs</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">[create alternative feature representations for acoustic modeling in automatic speech recognition] create alternative feature representations for acoustic modeling in automatic speech recognition</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">[sample distance] sample distance</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">[distance between anchor and positive sample to be smaller than distance between anchor and negative sample] positive sample is closer than negative sample</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">[meaningful descriptors] meaningful descriptors</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">[positive sample is closer than negative sample] positive sample is closer than negative sample</rdfs:comment>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A distance metric learning objective that optimizes the embedding space so that an anchor is closer to a positive example than to a negative example.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A distance-based objective function that encourages a sample to be closer to a positive example than to a negative one.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A distance-based objective function that encourages similar samples to be closer and dissimilar samples to be farther apart in embedding space.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A learning method that compares anchor, positive, and negative examples to learn meaningful descriptors from 3D point clouds.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A loss function that encourages a model to minimize the distance between an anchor and a positive sample while maximizing the distance to a negative sample.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A loss function that encourages the embedding of a positive sample to be closer to an anchor than to a negative sample.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A loss function that encourages the embedding of a positive sample to be closer to the anchor than a negative sample.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A loss function that encourages the model to map similar items closer together and dissimilar items further apart in a embedding space.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A loss function that trains a neural network to project signals into an embedding space where Euclidean distance represents perceptual dissimilarity.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A loss function used in embedding learning that encourages a positive sample to be closer to an anchor than a negative sample.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A loss function used in metric learning to ensure that a positive pair is closer than a negative pair by a specified margin.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A loss function used in metric learning to ensure that a positive sample is closer to the anchor than a negative sample by a certain margin.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A loss function used in metric learning to ensure that a reference sample is closer to a positive sample than to a negative one.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A loss function used in metric learning to ensure that an anchor sample is closer to a positive sample than to a negative sample in embedding space.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A loss function used in representation learning to ensure that a given sample is closer to a positive sample than to a negative sample.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A loss function used in unsupervised representation learning to ensure that similar samples are closer together than dissimilar ones in embedding space.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A loss function used to learn a compact Euclidean embedding where distance represents similarity between images for open-set recognition.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A loss function used to optimize the Euclidean distances between speaker embeddings to improve text-independent speaker verification.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A loss function used to train neural networks to learn embeddings that minimize the distance between matching pairs and maximize it for non-matching pairs.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A metric learning loss function that encourages the distance between an anchor and a positive sample to be smaller than the distance between an anchor and a negative sample.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A ranking loss function that encourages a model to minimize the distance between an anchor and a positive sample while maximizing it from a negative sample.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A self-supervised loss function used to create alternative feature representations for acoustic modeling in automatic speech recognition.</skos:definition>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://linqubit.co/ontology/ml_concepts#distance_based_loss"/>
    <ml:optimizes rdf:resource="http://linqubit.co/ontology/ml_concepts#embedding_space"/>
    <ml:learnsFrom rdf:resource="http://linqubit.co/ontology/ml_concepts#neural_network"/>
