    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A mathematical method for finding the minimum of a function where there is uncertainty in the objective or constraints.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A mathematical method for minimizing a loss function by using random samples of the gradient.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A mathematical method for minimizing an objective function by using random samples of the gradient.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A mathematical method for minimizing an objective function by using randomly sampled subsets of data to estimate the gradient.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A mathematical method for minimizing an objective function that involves random variables or noisy gradient estimates.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A mathematical method for minimizing an objective function using random samples of the gradient.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A mathematical method for minimizing objective functions using noisy gradient estimates to update model parameters.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A mathematical method that uses random variables to refine parameter estimates and minimize objective cost functions.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A mathematical process for finding critical points in non-convex functions using gradient and Hessian-vector products.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A mathematical process for finding the minimum of a function using random sampling to approximate gradients.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A mathematical process for finding the minimum of a function where the gradient is estimated from random samples.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A mathematical process of minimizing a loss function using random samples to find optimal parameters for a classifier.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A mathematical process used to minimize an objective function when the gradient is observed with noise.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A method for finding the minimum of a function that incorporates randomness to avoid local optima.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A method for improving the performance of learning algorithms by applying a secondary optimization process in a specific subspace.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A method for minimizing a function by using random samples to approximate the gradient or objective value.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A method for minimizing an expected loss function using random samples of the gradient.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A method for optimizing a model by introducing randomness into the output to maximize the expected value of a non-differentiable objective function.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A method for training neural networks that uses a dynamic learning rate schedule to adapt to the local geometry of the objective function.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A resource allocation process that minimizes regret by adapting to the complexity of the problem based on the regularity of returns.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">An iterative optimization process that uses stale stochastic gradients to find stationary points in non-convex smooth settings.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">An iterative process that uses noise or simulated annealing to find global minima of non-convex objective functions.</skos:definition>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://linqubit.co/ontology/ml_concepts#machine_learning"/>
