  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://linqubit.co/ontology/ml_concepts#decentralized_federated_learning">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Decentralized Federated Learning</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A collaborative machine learning approach where clients train models locally and synchronize updates via a peer-to-peer overlay network.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A distributed learning approach using a directed acyclic graph of model updates to enable personalization and poisoning robustness.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A distributed learning architecture that uses blockchain technology to train models without a central coordinator.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A distributed learning paradigm where model updates are aggregated via peer-to-peer consensus rather than a central server.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A distributed machine learning architecture that trains models across multiple participants without a central server, often utilizing blockchain for coordination.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A distributed machine learning framework where local users train models and exchange intermediate updates with neighbors without a central parameter server.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A distributed machine learning paradigm where clients collaborate to train a model without a central orchestrating server.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A distributed machine learning paradigm where models are trained across multiple nodes without a central coordinator.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A distributed training approach that utilizes node-to-node bandwidth via segmented gossip to synchronize models without a central server.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A distributed training approach where nodes perform local updates and communicate over a graph without a central server.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A distributed training architecture that allows multiple devices to collaboratively train a model without a central coordinating entity.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A distributed training paradigm that enables model synchronization across nodes without a central coordinator.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A peer-to-peer learning paradigm where devices coordinate model updates directly without the need for a central server.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A peer-to-peer machine learning setting where data is distributed among clients without a central server to orchestrate training.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A privacy-preserving framework where clients collaborate to train a global model via local communication without a central server.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A privacy-preserving machine learning setup where peers exchange model updates directly without a central coordinating server.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">[a directed acyclic graph of model updates] directed acyclic graph of model updates</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">[node-to-node bandwidth via segmented gossip] node-to-node bandwidth via segmented gossip</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">[model, global model] model</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">[model updates] model updates</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">[data] data</rdfs:comment>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A collaborative machine learning approach where clients train models locally and synchronize updates via a peer-to-peer overlay network.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A distributed learning approach using a directed acyclic graph of model updates to enable personalization and poisoning robustness.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A distributed learning architecture that uses blockchain technology to train models without a central coordinator.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A distributed learning paradigm where model updates are aggregated via peer-to-peer consensus rather than a central server.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A distributed machine learning architecture that trains models across multiple participants without a central server, often utilizing blockchain for coordination.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A distributed machine learning framework where local users train models and exchange intermediate updates with neighbors without a central parameter server.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A distributed machine learning paradigm where clients collaborate to train a model without a central orchestrating server.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A distributed machine learning paradigm where models are trained across multiple nodes without a central coordinator.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A distributed training approach that utilizes node-to-node bandwidth via segmented gossip to synchronize models without a central server.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A distributed training approach where nodes perform local updates and communicate over a graph without a central server.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A distributed training architecture that allows multiple devices to collaboratively train a model without a central coordinating entity.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A distributed training paradigm that enables model synchronization across nodes without a central coordinator.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A peer-to-peer learning paradigm where devices coordinate model updates directly without the need for a central server.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A peer-to-peer machine learning setting where data is distributed among clients without a central server to orchestrate training.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A privacy-preserving framework where clients collaborate to train a global model via local communication without a central server.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A privacy-preserving machine learning setup where peers exchange model updates directly without a central coordinating server.</skos:definition>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://linqubit.co/ontology/ml_concepts#federated_ml_protocol"/>
    <ml:hasComponent rdf:resource="http://linqubit.co/ontology/ml_concepts#blockchain"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://linqubit.co/ontology/ml_concepts#random_fourier_feature">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Random Fourier Feature</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A dimensionality reduction method that maps data into a finite Euclidean space to approximate a kernel function.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A dimensionality reduction technique that approximates kernel functions by mapping input data into a low-dimensional randomized feature space.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A dimensionality reduction technique that approximates kernel functions using a finite set of random basis functions.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A dimensionality reduction technique that maps input data into a randomized feature space to approximate kernel functions.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A low-dimensional feature representation used to approximate shift-invariant kernels for computationally efficient learning.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A method for approximating kernel functions by mapping input data into a low-dimensional randomized feature space.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A method for approximating shift-invariant kernels by mapping input data into a low-dimensional randomized feature space.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A method that approximates kernel functions to enable the scaling of kernel methods to large datasets.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A technique for kernel approximation that maps input data into a low-dimensional feature space to speed up kernel-based learning.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A technique that approximates a kernel function by projecting data into a low-dimensional random feature space.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">[a finite set of random basis functions] finite set of random basis functions</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">[approximate shift-invariant kernels for computationally efficient learning] approximate shift-invariant kernels for computationally efficient learning</rdfs:comment>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A dimensionality reduction method that maps data into a finite Euclidean space to approximate a kernel function.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A dimensionality reduction technique that approximates kernel functions by mapping input data into a low-dimensional randomized feature space.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A dimensionality reduction technique that approximates kernel functions using a finite set of random basis functions.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A dimensionality reduction technique that maps input data into a randomized feature space to approximate kernel functions.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A low-dimensional feature representation used to approximate shift-invariant kernels for computationally efficient learning.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A method for approximating kernel functions by mapping input data into a low-dimensional randomized feature space.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A method for approximating shift-invariant kernels by mapping input data into a low-dimensional randomized feature space.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A method that approximates kernel functions to enable the scaling of kernel methods to large datasets.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A technique for kernel approximation that maps input data into a low-dimensional feature space to speed up kernel-based learning.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A technique that approximates a kernel function by projecting data into a low-dimensional random feature space.</skos:definition>
