    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A supervised learning task that aims to identify whether a new sample belongs to a single known class or is an outlier.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A supervised learning task that builds a decision boundary using only samples from a single positive class.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A supervised learning task that identifies a target class by learning a boundary around a single set of representative samples.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A supervised learning task that identifies anomalous patterns by training a model to recognize the characteristics of a single normal class.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A supervised learning task that learns a decision boundary to distinguish normal instances from abnormal outliers.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A supervised learning task that trains a model using only normal samples to detect outliers.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A supervised learning task used for novelty or forgery detection by identifying samples that deviate from a single known class.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A supervised learning task where the model is trained on samples from only one class to identify outliers or anomalies.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A task that involves transforming data into a low-dimensional space for the ellipsoidal encapsulation of a single target class.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A type of anomaly detection that learns a boundary based exclusively on samples from a single normal class.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">An anomaly detection method that learns a boundary around a single class to distinguish its members from all other samples.</skos:definition>
