  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://linqubit.co/ontology/ml_concepts#automated_theorem_proving">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Automated Theorem Proving</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A computational field focused on the use of algorithms to prove mathematical conjectures.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A computational method for verifying mathematical conjectures through the systematic search for formal proofs.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A computational process that combines machine learning with formal logic to automate the discovery of mathematical proofs.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A computational process that uses formal logic and algorithms to verify the correctness of mathematical conjectures.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A computational process that uses formal logic and search algorithms to verify mathematical theorems.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A computational task that utilizes logic and search to verify mathematical conjectures.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A field of artificial intelligence dedicated to the computer-aided verification of mathematical theorems.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A task of using machine learning to generate tactics for interacting with proof assistants to prove mathematical theorems.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A task that uses statistical models and deep neural networks to synthesize proofs for mathematical propositions.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">An artificial intelligence task that uses neural generators to synthesize theorems and proofs to train a prover system.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">The use of computational algorithms to find formal proofs for mathematical conjectures.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">[formal logic and algorithms, formal logic and search algorithms, logic and search] logic and search</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">[statistical models and deep neural networks] statistical models and deep neural networks</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">[neural generators] neural generators</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">[tactics for interacting with proof assistants to prove mathematical theorems] tactics for interacting with proof assistants to prove mathematical theorems</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">[mathematical conjectures/theorems/proofs] mathematical conjectures/theorems/proofs</rdfs:comment>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A computational field focused on the use of algorithms to prove mathematical conjectures.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A computational method for verifying mathematical conjectures through the systematic search for formal proofs.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A computational process that combines machine learning with formal logic to automate the discovery of mathematical proofs.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A computational process that uses formal logic and algorithms to verify the correctness of mathematical conjectures.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A computational process that uses formal logic and search algorithms to verify mathematical theorems.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A computational task that utilizes logic and search to verify mathematical conjectures.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A field of artificial intelligence dedicated to the computer-aided verification of mathematical theorems.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A task of using machine learning to generate tactics for interacting with proof assistants to prove mathematical theorems.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A task that uses statistical models and deep neural networks to synthesize proofs for mathematical propositions.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">An artificial intelligence task that uses neural generators to synthesize theorems and proofs to train a prover system.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">The use of computational algorithms to find formal proofs for mathematical conjectures.</skos:definition>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://linqubit.co/ontology/ml_concepts#formal_knowledge_inference"/>
    <ml:hasComponent rdf:resource="http://linqubit.co/ontology/ml_concepts#machine_learning"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://linqubit.co/ontology/ml_concepts#generalized_linear_model">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Generalized Linear Model</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A flexible class of regression and classification models where the prediction is a linear combination of input variables.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A flexible class of regression models that relates a linear predictor to a response variable via a link function, often incorporating regularization.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A flexible class of statistical models that relates a response variable to linear predictors via a link function.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A flexible extension of ordinary linear regression that allows for response variables to have error distribution models other than a normal distribution.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A flexible extension of ordinary linear regression that allows for response variables with non-normal error distributions.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A flexible framework that extends traditional linear regression to allow for response variables with non-normal distributions.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A flexible generalization of ordinary linear regression that allows for response variables to have error distribution models other than a normal distribution.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A flexible generalization of ordinary linear regression that allows for response variables with error distribution models other than a normal distribution.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A flexible generalization of ordinary linear regression that allows for response variables with non-normal distributions.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A flexible generalization of ordinary linear regression that allows for response variables with non-normal error distributions.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A flexible linear model that allows the response variable to have an error distribution other than a normal distribution.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A flexible regression framework that allows the linear predictor to be related to the response variable via a link function.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A flexible regression framework that allows the response variable to have a distribution from the exponential family.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A flexible regression framework that allows the response variable to have an error distribution other than a normal distribution.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A flexible regression framework that extends linear regression to allow response variables to have error distribution models other than a normal distribution.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A flexible regression framework that extends ordinary linear regression to allow for response variables with non-normal distributions.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A flexible regression framework that extends ordinary linear regression to allow for response variables with non-normal error distributions.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A flexible regression framework that relates a linear predictor to a response variable via a link function.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A flexible regression framework that relates a response variable to linear predictors via a link function</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A flexible regression framework where the target variable is a function of a linear predictor.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A flexible regression model that allows the linear predictor to be related to the response variable via a link function.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A flexible statistical model that extends linear regression to allow for non-normal error distributions.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A mathematical framework used to characterize the asymptotic generalization error of single-layer neural networks with arbitrary non-linearities.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A statistical model that extends ordinary linear regression to allow for response variables with error distribution models other than a normal distribution.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A statistical model that relates a response variable to linear predictors using a link function to accommodate non-normal error distributions.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">[characterize the asymptotic generalization error of single-layer neural networks with arbitrary non-linearities] characterize the asymptotic generalization error of single-layer neural networks with arbitrary non-linearities</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">[linear combination of input variables] linear combination of input variables</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">[response variable to linear predictors, linear predictor to response variable via a link function] response variable to linear predictors</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">[response variables with non-normal error distributions, response variable to have a non-normal error distribution] response variables with non-normal error distributions</rdfs:comment>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A flexible class of regression and classification models where the prediction is a linear combination of input variables.</skos:definition>
