  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://linqubit.co/ontology/ml_concepts#sparse_regression">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Sparse Regression</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A linear approximation method that identifies a small subset of predictors to model the relationship between responsors and predictors.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A linear modeling technique that enforces a penalty on the number of non-zero coefficients to identify a small set of relevant predictors.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A machine learning strategy used for equation discovery by identifying a small number of relevant terms from a dictionary of operators.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A modeling technique that uses constraints to restrict the support of estimators for better interpretability and performance.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A regression technique that employs penalty terms to encourage sparsity in the model parameters to improve generalization.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A regression technique that identifies a small number of non-zero coefficients to produce a parsimonious model of the data.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A regression technique that identifies a small number of relevant features to produce a parsimonious model.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A regression technique that seeks a model with the fewest possible non-zero coefficients to ensure interpretability.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A regularization technique that produces models with few non-zero coefficients for high-dimensional data.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A statistical method that estimates a model where only a small number of coefficients are non-zero.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A statistical method that identifies a small subset of relevant features from a large library to discover underlying differential equations.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A statistical method that identifies a small subset of relevant features to model a response variable in high-dimensional data.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A statistical technique used to distill governing equations of dynamical systems by selecting a small subset of candidate functions.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A type of linear regression that seeks a solution with few non-zero coefficients to improve interpretability and generalization.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">[equation discovery by identifying a small number of relevant terms from a dictionary of operators] equation discovery by identifying a small number of relevant terms from a dictionary of operators</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">[constraints] constraints</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">[distill governing equations of dynamical systems by selecting a small subset of candidate functions] distill governing equations of dynamical systems by selecting a small subset of candidate functions</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">[penalty terms] penalty term</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">[small subset of predictors/features] small subset of predictors/features</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">[non-zero coefficients] non-zero coefficients</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">[model with few non-zero coefficients] non-zero coefficients</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">[models with few non-zero coefficients] non-zero coefficients</rdfs:comment>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A linear approximation method that identifies a small subset of predictors to model the relationship between responsors and predictors.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A linear modeling technique that enforces a penalty on the number of non-zero coefficients to identify a small set of relevant predictors.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A machine learning strategy used for equation discovery by identifying a small number of relevant terms from a dictionary of operators.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A modeling technique that uses constraints to restrict the support of estimators for better interpretability and performance.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A regression technique that employs penalty terms to encourage sparsity in the model parameters to improve generalization.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A regression technique that identifies a small number of non-zero coefficients to produce a parsimonious model of the data.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A regression technique that identifies a small number of relevant features to produce a parsimonious model.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A regression technique that seeks a model with the fewest possible non-zero coefficients to ensure interpretability.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A regularization technique that produces models with few non-zero coefficients for high-dimensional data.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A statistical method that estimates a model where only a small number of coefficients are non-zero.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A statistical method that identifies a small subset of relevant features from a large library to discover underlying differential equations.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A statistical method that identifies a small subset of relevant features to model a response variable in high-dimensional data.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A statistical technique used to distill governing equations of dynamical systems by selecting a small subset of candidate functions.</skos:definition>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A type of linear regression that seeks a solution with few non-zero coefficients to improve interpretability and generalization.</skos:definition>
