  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://linqubit.co/ontology/ml_concepts#rectified_linear_unit">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Rectified Linear Unit</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A function used as a classification mechanism in deep neural networks to threshold raw scores by zero.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A neural network activation function that outputs the input directly if it is positive, otherwise, it outputs zero.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A non-decreasing homogeneous activation function used in single-hidden-layer neural networks to enable non-linear variable selection.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A non-linear activation function that mitigates vanishing gradients and encourages sparsity in neural networks.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A non-linear activation function that outputs the input directly if it is above zero and otherwise outputs zero.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A non-linear activation function that outputs the input directly if it is positive and zero otherwise.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A non-linear activation function that outputs the input directly if it is positive, otherwise, it outputs zero.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A non-linear activation function used in deep neural networks to introduce sparsity and facilitate the training of deep architectures.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A non-linear activation function used in neural networks to introduce sparsity and facilitate training.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A non-linear activation function whose derivative at zero is treated as a tunable hyperparameter for optimization.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A non-smooth activation function that outputs the input directly if it is positive, otherwise, it outputs zero.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">A piece-wise linear activation function used in neural networks to introduce non-linearity while avoiding vanishing gradients.</rdfs:comment>
